    {
      "id": "markdown-2",
      "block": {
        "kind": "markdown",
        "text": "### Data-quality note\n\n**This data looks synthetic** (it resembles a Faker-style generated\ncustomer file), for these reasons:\n\n- **Flat sign-ups.** Every one of the 880 days from 2020-01-01 to\n  2022-05-29 has sign-ups, at an almost constant ~570 a day. The\n  month-to-month differences mostly reflect month length, not growth. The\n  trend line is nearly flat, and so is the weekday by month heatmap.\n  The peak month is simply a long month, not a real peak. The last month\n  (May 2022) is cut off at the 29th.\n- **Uniform categories.** Four of the five email TLDs (org, net, biz, info)\n  hold ~10% each and .com holds 60%. The 243 \"countries\" (including\n  Antarctica and Christmas Island) each get ~2,100 customers (about 0.43%);\n  only Korea and Congo have double that, probably because the source\n  country list has two entries for each.\n- **Company names** follow three templates in equal thirds: \"Surname Suffix\"\n  (LLC, Ltd, PLC, Inc, Group, \"and Sons\"), hyphenated surnames, and\n  \"A, B and C\". The last two have no legal form, so they fall in *Other*\n  (two thirds of customers).\n- **Emails are unrelated to people.** Only about 0.3% of addresses contain the\n  person's last name, and the email domain rarely matches the website.\n  71 rows repeat an email address already used by another customer.\n\nTreat the shape of this report as a demo of the tooling, not as business insight.\n"
      },
      "html": "<h3>Data-quality note</h3>\n<p><strong>This data looks synthetic</strong> (it resembles a Faker-style generated\ncustomer file), for these reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Flat sign-ups.</strong> Every one of the 880 days from 2020-01-01 to\n2022-05-29 has sign-ups, at an almost constant ~570 a day. The\nmonth-to-month differences mostly reflect month length, not growth. The\ntrend line is nearly flat, and so is the weekday by month heatmap.\nThe peak month is simply a long month, not a real peak. The last month\n(May 2022) is cut off at the 29th.</li>\n<li><strong>Uniform categories.</strong> Four of the five email TLDs (org, net, biz, info)\nhold ~10% each and .com holds 60%. The 243 \"countries\" (including\nAntarctica and Christmas Island) each get ~2,100 customers (about 0.43%);\nonly Korea and Congo have double that, probably because the source\ncountry list has two entries for each.</li>\n<li><strong>Company names</strong> follow three templates in equal thirds: \"Surname Suffix\"\n(LLC, Ltd, PLC, Inc, Group, \"and Sons\"), hyphenated surnames, and\n\"A, B and C\". The last two have no legal form, so they fall in <em>Other</em>\n(two thirds of customers).</li>\n<li><strong>Emails are unrelated to people.</strong> Only about 0.3% of addresses contain the\nperson's last name, and the email domain rarely matches the website.\n71 rows repeat an email address already used by another customer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Treat the shape of this report as a demo of the tooling, not as business insight.</p>\n"
    }
